export const DOCS: EmbeddedDoc[] = [{"id":"user","title":"User Guide","fileBase":"Inventory Analyzer - User Guide","markdown":"# Inventory Analyzer – User Guide\n\nVersion 1.2 · 24 Oct 2026 · Yasin Jariwala\n\nInventory Analyzer lists every file and folder inside a folder you choose, shows what uses the space, and exports the inventory to Excel or CSV. It runs on your computer and never changes, moves or uploads your files.\n\n## Quick start\n\nMost folders take seconds to scan. You'll have an Excel report in under a minute.\n\n1. Open the app: double-click **Start Inventory Analyzer.bat**, or open the published link.\n2. On **New Scan**, leave the defaults, or tick **Detect duplicate files** if you want to find copies.\n3. Click **Choose folder…** (Edge or Chrome) and pick the folder. In Firefox or Safari, or if the picker is blocked, click **Upload a folder…** instead. Nothing is actually uploaded.\n4. Watch the progress panel. Click **Cancel scan** at any time; the results so far are kept and marked partial.\n5. Review the **Dashboard**, then click **Excel (.xlsx)** or **All CSVs (.zip)** at the top right.\n\nNeed help later? Open **Help & Documents** in the left menu for this guide and the others, or download them as PDF or Word.\n\nWant to look around first? Click **Try with sample data** on New Scan. It runs the real engine on a made-up \"Shared Drive\" with about 320 files.\n\n## See which folder has how many files, and where each file is\n\nOpen **Folder Explorer** (new in v1.1). It shows your folder as a tree, like Windows Explorer, with two counts on every folder.\n\n| On a folder row | Means |\n| --- | --- |\n| **N files here** (blue) | Files sitting directly in that folder |\n| **N in total** (grey) | Files in that folder plus all of its subfolders |\n| empty / no files (only empty subfolders) | Nothing to count inside |\n| couldn't be opened | The browser was denied access, so the contents are unknown |\n\n1. Click **▸** next to a folder to open it. You'll see its subfolders first, then its files with size and modified date.\n2. Use **Expand all** to open every folder at once, or **Collapse all** to start over.\n3. To find where a file is, type part of its name in **Find a file or folder**. Only matching items and the folders that contain them stay visible, highlighted, with a match count.\n4. From the **Dashboard**, the **Where your files are** table lists each top-level folder by number of files. Click one to open it in the explorer.\n5. From **File Inventory**, click the pin icon next to any file's path to jump to that file in the tree.\n\nThe Excel report has the same tree on its **Folder Tree** sheet, indented by level, with Files here and Files in total columns.\n\n## Go back to an earlier scan and download its reports\n\nEvery scan is saved on this PC (new in v1.2), so you can come back to it after closing the app.\n\n1. Click **Scan History**. Each row shows the folder name, scan ID, date, start → end time, status, file and folder counts, and size.\n2. Click **Open** to reload that scan. Every screen works as if it had just run, including the Folder Explorer and all downloads. A note on the Dashboard says it is a saved scan, showing the folder as it was then.\n3. Or click **Excel** or **CSVs** to download that scan's reports without opening it.\n4. When the app starts, **New Scan** offers **Open last scan** for your most recent saved scan.\n\nFull results are kept for the newest 20 scans; older ones keep only a summary. Scans made before v1.2 show **Summary only** and can't be reopened, so rescan those folders once. To keep only summaries, turn off **Keep full results of past scans on this PC** in Settings.\n\n## Scan options\n\nSet these on New Scan before you choose a folder. Your choices are remembered, except duplicate detection, which starts off each session.\n\n| Option | Default | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Include dot-prefixed items | On | Includes names starting with \".\" (e.g. `.git`, `.env`). Turned off, they are skipped and counted under \"skipped\". Browsers don't expose the Windows \"hidden\" attribute. |\n| Detect duplicate files (SHA-256) | Off | Reads same-size files and compares their content hashes. This is slower on big folders, and contents are never stored. |\n| Large-file threshold | 100 MB | Files at or above this size appear in the Large files list and key findings. |\n| Top-N report size | 25 | How many items the Largest files and Largest folders lists show. |\n| Hashing size limit (Settings) | 1,024 MB | Files above this aren't hashed and are marked \"unverified\". |\n\n## The screens\n\nResult screens unlock after your first scan. The left menu stays locked while a scan runs.\n\n| Screen | What it shows | What you can do |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Dashboard | Files, folders, total size, average size, largest file and folder, duration, status, errors; Where your files are (files per top-level folder); four charts; key findings | Jump to errors; download reports |\n| Folder Explorer | Your folder as a tree: files here and in total on every folder; each file listed under its folder | Open/close folders; expand or collapse all; find a file or folder by name |\n| File Inventory | One row per file: name, relative path, parent folder, extension, size, modified date, dot-prefixed, status, duplicate status | Search; filter by extension, folder, size (MB), date and duplicate status; sort any column; change rows per page |\n| Folder Summary | One row per folder: depth, direct and recursive files, subfolders and size, contents, completeness | Search and filter; click a folder name to list only that folder's files |\n| Storage Analysis | Top-N largest files and folders, extension table, large files, size by depth, folders with only subfolders | Change Top-N and the large-file threshold on the spot |\n| Duplicate Files | Duplicate groups, copies, redundant storage, unverified count | Expand a group to see every path |\n| Empty Folders | Folders with no files anywhere inside | Review before a cleanup |\n| Scan Errors | Every item that couldn't be read, with a category and a suggested fix | Filter by category |\n| Scan History | Every scan on this PC: folder, scan ID, date and times, status, counts, size, errors | Open a saved scan; download its Excel or CSVs; delete one, or clear all |\n| Settings | Defaults, hashing limit, local data, privacy and limitations | Restore defaults; clear stored history |\n| Help & Documents | This User Guide, the Setup & Administration Guide and the Release Notes, inside the app | Read them; download each as PDF or Word |\n\n## Reading the results\n\n**Direct** counts only what sits immediately inside a folder. **Recursive** counts everything below it. Each file is counted once in the totals.\n\nExample: `Project/docs` holds 2 files (300 KB) and a subfolder `old` with 3 files (700 KB). Docs then shows 2 files and 300 KB direct, and 5 files and 1,000 KB recursive.\n\n| Label | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| Complete | Everything inside the folder was read |\n| Incomplete | Something inside couldn't be read, or the scan was cancelled. Totals are a lower bound. |\n| Empty | No files and no subfolders |\n| Empty tree | No files anywhere — only empty subfolders |\n| Subfolders only | No files directly inside, but files exist deeper down |\n| Unknown | The folder couldn't be opened, so its contents are unknown (never shown as empty) |\n| Duplicate #n | Same size and same SHA-256 content as other files in group n, whatever their names |\n| Unique | No other file has the same content (or the same size) |\n| Unverified | Couldn't be hashed (unreadable, or over the hashing limit) |\n\nSizes are logical file sizes, not disk space used. Compression, cluster size and cloud placeholders (OneDrive, iCloud) can make real disk usage differ.\n\n## Reports you can download\n\nThe Excel workbook and the CSV files contain the same data. Before you download, the app checks that every row count matches the scan, then confirms it on screen.\n\n| Excel sheet | CSV file | Contents |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Executive Summary | — | Root, scan ID, mode, status, start/end, duration, totals, settings, key findings, row-count reconciliation |\n| Folder Tree | `folder_tree.csv` | Every folder and file in tree order, indented by level: type, files here, files in total, size, relative path |\n| File Inventory | `file_inventory.csv` | Every file with path, parent, extension, size in bytes and formatted, modified date, status (+ duplicate status and SHA-256 when enabled) |\n| Folder Summary | `folder_summary.csv` | Every folder, including the root, with direct and recursive counts and sizes, empty status, completeness |\n| Extension Summary | `extension_summary.csv` | Per extension: file count, total size, % of total, average, largest (Excel adds a totals row) |\n| Largest Files | `largest_files.csv` | Top-N files by size |\n| Largest Folders | `largest_folders.csv` | Top-N folders by recursive size |\n| Duplicate Files | `duplicate_files.csv` | Only when duplicate detection was on: group, hash, path, size, copies |\n| Empty Folders | `empty_folders.csv` | Empty and empty-tree folders with their classification |\n| Scan Errors | `scan_errors.csv` | Every error with path, type, category, message and time |\n\n- Use **Single CSV…** to download just one file, or **All CSVs (.zip)** for all of them.\n- Excel sheets have frozen headers and filters. Tables over 1,048,575 rows are split into numbered sheets, e.g. \"File Inventory (2)\"; nothing is dropped.\n- CSV values starting with = + − or @ get a leading ' so Excel never runs them as formulas.\n\n## Good to know\n\n- **Is anything uploaded?** No. Even \"Upload a folder\" only gives this page read access. Everything stays on your computer.\n- **Can it delete or tidy files?** No. It is read-only by design and only reports.\n- **Why is there no full path like C:\\\\…?** Browsers don't reveal it. Paths start at the folder you picked.\n- **Why only a modified date?** Browsers don't expose created or accessed dates.\n- **Empty folders missing in Firefox or Safari?** Upload mode can't see empty folders. Use Edge or Chrome.\n- **How big a folder can I scan?** Up to a few hundred thousand files works well on a typical laptop. For bigger trees, scan subfolders separately.\n- **Where did my last scan go?** In Scan History. Click Open to reload it, or download its reports from its row. Browsers can clear this storage, so export reports you must keep.\n- **Where are downloaded reports saved?** In your browser's Downloads folder (normally `C:\\Users\\<you>\\Downloads`).\n- **Comparing two scans** is planned for a later release.\n","pdf":"","docx":""},{"id":"setup","title":"Setup & Administration Guide","fileBase":"Inventory Analyzer - Setup and Administration Guide","markdown":"# Inventory Analyzer – Setup & Administration Guide\n\nVersion 1.2 · 24 Oct 2026 · Yasin Jariwala\n\nInventory Analyzer v1.2 runs entirely in the browser. On any Windows PC with Microsoft Edge, double-click **Start Inventory Analyzer.bat**. There is nothing to install, no server, and no admin rights are needed.\n\n## Ways to run it\n\nUse the desktop launcher for real work. Use the published link to demo or try it, and the developer build only to change the code.\n\n| Mode | Best for | You need | Folder picker | Scan history | Downloads |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Desktop launcher (`Start Inventory Analyzer.bat` + `Inventory Analyzer.html`) | Everyday use on a Windows PC | Microsoft Edge (built into Windows 10/11) | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Published link on claude.ai | Demos, quick checks, sharing with colleagues | The link must be shared (Share → Anyone with the link); until then others see \"Sign in to view this page\" | May be blocked by the hosted page — use Upload a folder | Yes | Yes, after you confirm each save |\n| Developer build (`npm run dev`) | Changing code, running tests | Node.js 18 or later | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n\nAll three run the same app and produce identical reports. Each keeps its own scan history.\n\n## Install on a Windows PC\n\nSetup takes about a minute and needs no admin rights.\n\n1. Copy two files into any folder, e.g. `C:\\Users\\<you>\\Tools\\Inventory Analyzer\\`:\n    - `Start Inventory Analyzer.bat`\n    - `Inventory Analyzer.html`\n2. Double-click **Start Inventory Analyzer.bat**. The app opens in its own Microsoft Edge window.\n3. Optional: right-click the .bat file → **Send to → Desktop (create shortcut)**. Rename the shortcut \"Inventory Analyzer\".\n4. If Edge isn't available, the launcher opens the .html file in your default browser instead. You can also open the .html file directly in Chrome or Edge.\n5. First run: choose **Try with sample data** to confirm the reports and Excel export work, then scan a real folder.\n\nKeep the two files in the same folder. The launcher looks for `Inventory Analyzer.html` next to itself.\n\n## Browser support\n\nChromium browsers (Edge, Chrome, Opera) support every feature. Firefox and Safari use the folder-upload mode, which can't see empty folders.\n\n| Browser | Choose folder (picker) | Upload a folder | Empty-folder detection | Duplicate detection |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Microsoft Edge (Windows 10/11) | Yes | Yes | Yes (picker mode) | Yes |\n| Google Chrome (desktop) | Yes | Yes | Yes (picker mode) | Yes |\n| Firefox | No | Yes | No | Yes |\n| Safari (macOS) | No | Yes | No | Yes |\n| Mobile browsers | No | Varies | No | Varies |\n\nThe app checks the browser on the New Scan screen (**Browser capability check**) and disables any option it can't support.\n\n## Data, privacy and local storage\n\nThe app sends no data anywhere and never changes your files. Folders are opened read-only.\n\n| Data | Where it lives | How long | How to remove it |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| The open scan's file list and hashes (before it is saved) | Browser memory only | Until the tab or window closes | Close the window |\n| Scan summaries, plus full results (file and folder names, paths, sizes, dates — never contents) for the newest 20 scans | Browser IndexedDB on this PC | Until cleared, or until the browser evicts site data | Scan History → **Clear all history**, or Settings → **Clear stored scan history** |\n| Default scan options | Browser local storage on this PC | Until changed or browser data is cleared | Settings → **Restore defaults** |\n| Exported reports (.xlsx, .csv, .zip) | Wherever you save them (default: Downloads) | Your choice | Delete the files |\n\n- File contents are read only to compute SHA-256 hashes when duplicate detection is on. They are never stored.\n- There is no telemetry, no analytics and no network call carrying scan data. The automated tests check this.\n- Private/InPrivate windows and \"clear browsing data\" remove scan history. Export reports you must keep. To store only summaries, turn off Settings → Keep full results of past scans on this PC.\n\n## Distributing and updating\n\nTo give the app to colleagues, send them the two files or share the published link. To update it, replace `Inventory Analyzer.html`.\n\n- **Share the files:** zip `Start Inventory Analyzer.bat` and `Inventory Analyzer.html` and send them by email or Teams, or place them on a shared drive. Some mail filters block .bat files. If yours does, send only the .html file and ask people to open it in Edge or Chrome.\n- **Share the link:** the Inventory Analyzer app is private until you open it on claude.ai and choose **Share → Anyone with the link**. Until then, anyone else sees \"Sign in to view this page\". The guides are built into the app (Help & Documents), so one link gives people everything.\n- **Update:** overwrite `Inventory Analyzer.html` with the new build. The launcher doesn't change, and saved scan history and settings are kept.\n- **Rebuild from source** (project folder `folder-file-inventory-analyzer`):\n    1. `npm install`\n    2. `npm run build:single` → `dist-single/index.html`\n    3. Copy that file over `Inventory Analyzer.html`.\n- The Settings → About panel shows the version; the current release is **v1.2**.\n\n## Verifying a build\n\nv1.2 passes all 97 automated tests, covering 44 user scenarios. Rerun them before you distribute a new build.\n\n| Suite | Command | Tests | What it proves |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit | `npm run test:unit` | 42 | Counts, direct and recursive sizes, duplicates, errors, cancellation, folder tree, history storage, Excel and CSV correctness |\n| UI scenarios | `npm run test:ui` | 47 | Every user scenario driven through the real app with clicks and typing |\n| End-to-end | `npm run test:e2e` | 8 | Production build in Chromium: real folder scan, downloads reopened, no network, phone layout, keyboard use, large scan reopened after reload |\n| Everything + coverage | `npm run test:all` | 97 | Writes coverage to `test-reports/coverage` (97.7% of statements) |\n\nBefore the first end-to-end run, install the test browser once with `npx playwright install chromium`. The scenario list is in `tests/SCENARIOS.md` and the results are in `test-reports/TEST_REPORT.md`.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Double-clicking the .bat does nothing | Edge missing, or the .html isn't next to the .bat | Keep both files in one folder; open the .html in Chrome directly |\n| Windows shows \"Windows protected your PC\" | SmartScreen warning for a downloaded .bat | Click **More info → Run anyway**, or open the .html directly |\n| **Choose folder…** is greyed out | Firefox/Safari, or the browser lacks the folder picker | Use **Upload a folder**, or switch to Edge/Chrome |\n| \"The folder picker is blocked in this context\" | Opened inside the published page on claude.ai | Use **Upload a folder**, or the desktop launcher |\n| Many `permission-denied` errors | System or protected folders inside the selection | Select a narrower folder |\n| `not-readable` on some files | File open or locked in another app (Outlook PST, open Office files) | Close the app and re-scan |\n| \"Scanned on the main thread\" notice | Background worker not allowed (e.g. opened from disk) | No action needed; results are identical |\n| Scan History shows \"Summary only\" | Scan made before v1.2, older than the newest 20, or full results turned off | Rescan the folder |\n| Scan History is empty after reopening | Private window, browser data cleared, or a different mode (launcher vs link) | Use a normal window and the same mode; export reports to keep results |\n| Browser slows down on a huge folder | Hundreds of thousands of files held in memory | Scan subfolders separately; prefer the CSV ZIP export |\n| Excel export fails | Out of memory on a very large inventory | Use **All CSVs (.zip)** |\n","pdf":"","docx":""},{"id":"release","title":"Release Notes","fileBase":"Inventory Analyzer - Release Notes","markdown":"# Inventory Analyzer – Release Notes\n\nVersions 1.0 – 1.2 · 24 Oct 2026 · Yasin Jariwala\n\nInventory Analyzer is a browser app that inventories any folder you choose, analyzes storage, and exports Excel and CSV reports. It runs locally and uploads nothing. The current release, v1.2, passes all 97 automated tests.\n\n## What's new in v1.2 (24 Oct 2026)\n\nv1.2 lets you go back to an earlier scan and download its reports, even after closing the app, and carries its own guides. All 97 automated tests pass, up from 81.\n\n| Change | Where |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Open** any saved scan: every screen, the Folder Explorer and all exports work as if it had just run | Scan History |\n| **Excel / CSVs** downloads for any saved scan, without opening it | Scan History |\n| **Open last scan** offer when the app starts | New Scan |\n| Redesigned history table: full folder names, scan ID, start → end times and buttons no longer cut off | Scan History |\n| **Keep full results of past scans on this PC** (on by default; newest 20 scans) | Settings |\n| **Help & Documents**: User Guide, Setup & Administration Guide and Release Notes inside the app, each downloadable as PDF or Word | New menu item |\n\nScans made before v1.2 kept only a summary, so they show as \"Summary only\" and can't be reopened. Rescan those folders once to save them.\n\nTested at size: a 26,160-file, 22,000-folder scan reopened in 0.4 s after a page reload, and its Excel rebuilt from history in 6.2 s.\n\n## What's new in v1.1 (24 Oct 2026)\n\nv1.1 answers \"which folder has how many files, and where is each file?\" All 81 automated tests pass, up from 70.\n\n| Change | Where |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Folder Explorer**: an expandable folder tree with \"files here\" and \"in total\" counts on every folder, and each file listed under its folder | New menu item after Dashboard |\n| Find a file or folder by name: only matches and their folders show, with a match count | Folder Explorer |\n| **Where your files are**: files and size per top-level folder; click to open in the explorer | Dashboard |\n| Show in folder tree: jump from any file to its spot in the tree | File Inventory |\n| **Folder Tree** sheet: the whole tree indented by level, with files here and in total | Excel (2nd sheet) and `folder_tree.csv` |\n\nBuilt test-first: four new scenarios (S35–S38) were written as tests and failed (14 failing tests) before the feature was built.\n\n## What's in v1.0\n\n| Area | Feature | PRD |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Folder access | Read-only folder picker (Edge/Chrome); folder-upload fallback (Firefox/Safari); browser capability check | FR-01 |\n| Scanning | Recursive inventory in a background worker; progress, elapsed time, honest % and ETA; cancel with partial results kept | FR-02, FR-11 |\n| Calculations | Direct vs recursive file counts, folder counts and sizes; completeness per folder; each file counted once | FR-03 |\n| Dashboard | KPIs, 4 charts, key findings, status and error banner | FR-04 |\n| File Inventory | Search; filters for extension, folder, size, date and duplicates; sorting; paging; relative path and dot-prefixed columns | FR-05 |\n| Folder Summary | Per-folder direct/recursive metrics; click through to a folder's files | FR-06 |\n| Storage Analysis | Top-N files and folders, extension table, large files, size by depth, subfolder-only folders | FR-07 |\n| Duplicates (optional) | Size grouping, then SHA-256 confirmation; redundant bytes; unverified handling | FR-08 |\n| Exports | 9-sheet Excel workbook; 8 CSVs; ZIP bundle; formula-injection protection; row-count reconciliation; sheet splitting over 1,048,575 rows | FR-09, FR-10 |\n| Errors | Categorized errors with fixes; folders that can't be read marked unknown, never empty | FR-12 |\n| History and settings | Local scan history (delete/clear); remembered defaults | FR-13 |\n| Delivery | Desktop launcher for Windows (no install); published link; developer build | — |\n\n## Quality\n\nv1.0 was built test-first against 34 user scenarios taken from the PRD. All 70 automated tests passed, covering 97.5% of statements and 89.3% of branches.\n\n| Suite | Tests | Result |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Unit (engine, exports, history, worker) | 34 | Pass |\n| UI scenarios (real clicks and typing) | 30 | Pass |\n| End-to-end (production build in Chromium) | 6 | Pass |\n\nWriting the tests before fixing anything exposed 8 defects, all fixed in v1.0:\n\n| Defect found by tests | Fix in v1.0 |\n| --- | --- |\n| Duplicates never confirmed on browsers without `Blob.arrayBuffer` (Safari < 14) | Hashing falls back to FileReader |\n| File Inventory lacked relative path and dot-prefixed columns | Columns added |\n| Opening `docs` also listed `docs-archive` | Drill-down matches the exact subtree |\n| Number boxes couldn't be cleared and retyped (typing 10 gave 110) | New number field |\n| Declining a save showed \"Export failed\" | Now says \"Save cancelled\" |\n| Scan History lacked scan ID and completion time | Columns added |\n| Page buttons had no accessible names | Labels added for screen readers |\n| CSV ZIP could fill with junk entries | Data normalized before zipping |\n\n## Known limitations\n\n- **Upload mode can't see empty folders.** Use Edge or Chrome for full empty-folder detection.\n- **The published link may block the folder picker.** Use Upload a folder, or the desktop launcher.\n- **The inventory is held in memory.** A few hundred thousand files is the practical ceiling on a typical laptop.\n- **Only last-modified dates are available.** Browsers don't expose full disk paths, created or accessed dates, or the OS hidden attribute.\n- **Sizes are logical file sizes,** not space used on disk.\n- **Duplicate hashing reads each candidate file whole.** Files over the hashing limit are marked unverified.\n- **Each mode keeps its own history.** Scans made in the desktop launcher don't appear in the published link, and vice versa.\n- **Testing scope:** end-to-end tests ran in Chromium only, and the native picker was tested with a stand-in.\n\n## Planned next\n\n- Scan comparison: what changed between two scans (deferred from the MVP by PRD decision)\n- Streaming very large inventories to local storage instead of memory\n- Virtualized tables for faster scrolling through huge lists\n- End-to-end tests in Firefox and Safari\n","pdf":"","docx":""}];
